---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import MctsExplainer from '../../../nn-chess/ui/MctsExplainer.tsx';

// The body is a tagged template string because Astro parses `{...}` in
// markup as JSX expressions; nearly every LaTeX fragment here contains
// braces (e.g. `\sum_{a'}`), and writing them inline would force every
// brace to be escaped as `&#123;`. Keeping the content in a single
// `set:html` payload avoids that and keeps the math human-readable.
const intro = String.raw`
  <h1>How nn-chess thinks: PUCT-style MCTS, sims/move, and cPuct</h1>
  <p><a href="/nn-chess">← back to play</a></p>

  <p>
    Every time the nn-chess opponent picks a move, it runs a small
    <strong>Monte Carlo Tree Search</strong> (MCTS) rooted at the current
    position. This page is a long-form, math-first explainer of what
    that search does, why it does it that way, and what the two knobs
    on the main page —
    <em>sims/move</em> and <em>cPuct</em> — actually control. The end of
    the page is an interactive walk-through of a mate-in-2 that uses
    your own trained networks to drive each MCTS call.
  </p>

  <h2>1. The shape of the search</h2>
  <p>
    Classical full-width minimax explores every legal move to a fixed
    depth and is hopeless for chess: with a branching factor around 35
    and a value network costing real wall-clock time per evaluation,
    even depth-6 minimax dwarfs the per-move budget we have in the
    browser. MCTS gives up exhaustive coverage in exchange for a budget
    we can dial: it spends $n$ simulations exploring the tree, and the
    tree it builds is highly asymmetric — deep along promising lines,
    shallow elsewhere.
  </p>
  <p>
    The flavour we use is the <strong>PUCT</strong> variant from AlphaGo
    Zero / AlphaZero, where a single neural network supplies both a
    <em>prior policy</em> $P(s, a)$ — a probability distribution over
    moves from each state $s$ — and a <em>value</em> $V(s)\in[-1, 1]$
    estimating how good $s$ is for the side to move. The terminal
    reward is fixed: $+1$ for the side that delivered checkmate, $-1$
    for the side that was checkmated, $0$ for any draw. <em>That is the
    only chess heuristic baked in.</em> Everything else has to be learnt
    by the network from games.
  </p>

  <p>One simulation has four phases, run repeatedly for the search budget:</p>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>Selection.</strong> Starting at the root, descend through the
        tree by repeatedly picking the edge with the highest PUCT score
        (see below) until you reach a node that has either never been
        expanded or that is a terminal state.</li>
    <li><strong>Expansion.</strong> If the leaf is non-terminal, ask the
        policy net for $P(\cdot \mid s)$ and $V(s)$. Mask $P$ to the
        legal moves at $s$ and renormalise. Create one child edge per
        legal move and store its prior.</li>
    <li><strong>Evaluation.</strong> Use $V(s)$ as the value to back up.
        If the leaf is terminal, use the terminal reward instead — no
        rollout, no playout policy. AlphaZero showed that rollouts are
        not necessary once you have a competent value head.</li>
    <li><strong>Backup.</strong> Walk back up the path you came down,
        incrementing visit counts and accumulating value along each
        edge. The value flips sign at every step because each edge
        crosses from one side to the other; what's good for one player
        is bad for the other.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    After all simulations finish, the move chosen at the root is the
    one with the most visits. The visit-count distribution itself is
    also the policy target used to train the network (AlphaZero's
    self-play loop pushes the policy net toward the improved policy
    that MCTS produces from its priors).
  </p>

  <h2>2. The PUCT formula in detail</h2>

  <p>
    Each edge $(s, a)$ in the search tree carries four numbers we
    update over the search:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>$P(s, a)$ — the prior from the policy net, fixed at expansion time.</li>
    <li>$N(s, a)$ — visit count.</li>
    <li>$W(s, a)$ — total backed-up value through this edge.</li>
    <li>$Q(s, a) = W(s, a) / N(s, a)$ — average value when $N(s, a) > 0$, else $0$.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Selection at $s$ picks the legal action $a$ that maximises:</p>

  $$
    \mathrm{PUCT}(s, a) \;=\; \underbrace{Q(s, a)}_{\text{exploit}}
    \;+\; \underbrace{c_{\text{puct}} \cdot P(s, a) \cdot \dfrac{\sqrt{N(s)}}{1 + N(s, a)}}_{\text{explore } U(s,a)}
  $$

  <p>
    where $N(s) = \sum_{a'} N(s, a')$. Both terms are interpretable:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Exploit.</strong> $Q(s, a)$ is just the average outcome we've
      seen through this edge so far, in $[-1, 1]$ from the perspective
      of the side to move at $s$. If the network thinks one edge
      consistently wins, its $Q$ trends positive and the search
      gravitates there.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Explore.</strong> $U(s, a)$ is large when (a) we have a high
      prior — the policy net likes this move — and (b) we have not
      visited it much yet. As $N(s, a) \to \infty$ for a particular
      edge, its $U$ decays as $1/N(s, a)$ while $Q$ stays bounded; the
      exploration bonus eventually vanishes and selection is dominated
      by exploitation.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Why $\sqrt{N(s)}$ in the numerator? You can derive a closely
    related bound from UCB1 for stochastic bandits, but the AlphaGo Zero
    paper just settled on this form because it works: the search
    distributes visits across edges in rough proportion to
    $\sqrt{N(s)} \cdot P(s, a)$ early on (when $N(s, a)$ is small and
    $Q$ is noisy), then concentrates on whichever edges develop a high
    $Q$. The numerator grows slowly enough that the explore bonus
    doesn't blow up, but fast enough that several alternatives still
    get sampled per node.
  </p>

  <p>
    All ties on $Q + U$ are broken deterministically (high prior first,
    then lexicographic UCI). Search is otherwise purely deterministic
    once the policy net and rng seed are fixed — see the determinism
    test in <code>core/mcts/mcts.test.ts</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>3. The backup, with sign-flipping</h2>

  <p>
    During backup, the value $v$ produced at the leaf travels back up
    the descent path. Two-player, zero-sum games are an alternating
    setting: positions on adjacent plies belong to opposite players, so
    a value that is good for the leaf's mover is bad for the parent's
    mover by exactly the same amount. We encode this by negating $v$
    every step:
  </p>

  $$
    v \;\leftarrow\; -v, \quad
    N(s, a) \mathrel{+}= 1, \quad
    W(s, a) \mathrel{+}= v
  $$

  <p>
    This convention keeps every $Q(s, a)$ in the tree measured from the
    side-to-move's perspective at $s$, which is exactly what selection
    needs to compare candidate moves consistently. The terminal handling
    in <code>core/mcts/index.ts</code> does the same flip: if a child
    state is terminal, the value backed up at that child is
    $-\textsf{terminalValueForMover}(s)$ — because
    <code>terminalValueForMover</code> answers from the perspective of
    the side that <em>just moved</em>, not the side to move now.
  </p>

  <h2>4. What sims/move actually controls</h2>

  <p>
    The <em>sims/move</em> slider on the main page caps the number of
    PUCT simulations the search runs before picking a move. A few
    quantitative things follow from increasing it:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Visit distribution sharpens.</strong> In the limit
      $n \to \infty$, MCTS's visit-count distribution
      $\pi_n(a) = N(s, a) / n$ approaches the distribution that puts
      all its mass on the optimal action under the value model. AlphaZero
      then samples training-time moves as $\pi(a) \propto N(s, a)^{1/T}$
      for temperature $T$, but our deployed opponent uses $T = 0$
      (greedy argmax) so what matters is just which edge wins the
      visit count.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Forced mates become discoverable.</strong> A mate-in-$k$
      from the side to move requires the search to reach a terminal
      mating leaf along at least one defensive subtree at every
      intermediate ply. With branching factor $b$, the worst-case
      number of simulations to discover a mate-in-$k$ from a fresh-init
      net is roughly $b^k$ — the search has to fail down many wrong
      subtrees before the terminal $+1$ propagates back to the root.
      In practice the search is much more efficient than $b^k$ because
      PUCT clusters visits on a small subset of moves at each ply, but
      this exponential is why doubling sims/move has such an outsized
      effect on tactical strength.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cost is linear.</strong> Each simulation costs one
      forward pass of the policy net (in the worst case — repeated
      states are cached within a single search via
      <code>repetitionKey</code>) plus $O(\text{depth})$ tree
      bookkeeping. Doubling sims/move doubles wall-clock per move.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Diminishing returns once the search is informed.</strong>
      Once the policy net has $Q$ that already orders moves well, extra
      sims mostly reinforce the same top edge. The interesting regime
      for nn-chess is the cold-start one, where doubling sims/move
      actually lets the terminal $+1$ from a mating line reach the root
      that it couldn't reach before.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>5. What cPuct actually controls</h2>

  <p>
    $c_{\text{puct}}$ is a single scalar that determines how much weight
    selection puts on $U$ relative to $Q$. The two boundary cases are
    instructive:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>$c_{\text{puct}} \to 0$:</strong> the explore term vanishes
      and selection becomes "pick the edge with the highest $Q$, breaking
      ties on $N$." With $Q$ initialised to $0$ everywhere, this
      degenerates to "always pick the same first-tried move," because
      after one visit that edge has a nonzero $Q$ (from its child's $V$)
      and every other edge has $Q = 0$. Useful only after the search has
      already converged.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>$c_{\text{puct}} \to \infty$:</strong> $Q$ becomes
      negligible and selection picks
      $a = \mathrm{argmax}_a P(s, a) / (1 + N(s, a))$. This sweeps
      through moves roughly in prior order without ever exploiting the
      backed-up values. Search behaves like pure rollout-budget
      apportionment by the prior and never converges on the best move.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    AlphaZero used $c_{\text{puct}} = 1.5$ for chess; that's the
    nn-chess default. The right value depends on (a) the value-head
    scale (ours is $\tanh$, so $Q \in [-1, 1]$) and (b) how peaked the
    prior is. A near-uniform prior — which is what you get from a
    cold-start net — calls for a smaller $c_{\text{puct}}$, because $U$
    is then much flatter and adding more of it just spreads visits
    across edges $Q$ has already ranked. A very peaked prior calls for
    a larger $c_{\text{puct}}$ to overcome the policy's confidence when
    it's wrong.
  </p>

  <p>
    Concretely on the demo below: with the cold-start nets nn-chess
    ships with, a value of $c_{\text{puct}}$ around $1$–$1.5$ is enough
    that the terminal signal from <code>Qd7+ Kf8 Qf7#</code> can
    propagate back through MCTS. Push it up to $4$ and you'll see
    visits scatter across many root moves and the mate becomes harder
    to land at low sim counts. Push it down to $0.25$ and you'll see
    the search lock onto whichever queen move happened to get the first
    above-zero $Q$.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Dirichlet noise (briefly)</h2>

  <p>
    The MCTS module supports root-only Dirichlet noise:
    $\tilde P(s_0, a) = (1 - \varepsilon) P(s_0, a) + \varepsilon \eta_a$
    where $\eta \sim \mathrm{Dir}(\alpha)$. This is how AlphaZero forces
    a bit of exploration during self-play (it keeps the data generator
    from collapsing onto a single line). We use it in self-play games
    against the imitator with the standard chess values
    $\alpha = 0.3$, $\varepsilon = 0.25$; we do <em>not</em> use it
    during play against the user — at inference time we want the best
    move, not an exploration-noised one. See
    <code>SELFPLAY_SEARCH</code> in
    <code>nn-chess/ui/opponent-config.ts</code> for where this is wired
    in. The demo below also leaves Dirichlet noise off so the visit
    counts you see are pure PUCT.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Interactive demo: a mate-in-2 against your own nets</h2>

  <p>
    The walkthrough below loads your stored opponent and imitator
    networks from this browser's IndexedDB (the same ones the main page
    plays against and trains). At each position it runs MCTS with the
    sims-per-move and cPuct you set, and lists the top candidate moves
    with their $N$, $Q$, $P$, and the
    $U = c_{\text{puct}} \cdot P \cdot \sqrt{N(s)} / (1 + N(s, a))$
    that the <em>next</em> simulation would add. Click any candidate
    row to play that move and see MCTS think about the resulting
    position; the demo goes up to four half-moves deep so you can walk
    the full forced sequence and one extra ply of slack for offline
    exploration.
  </p>

  <p>
    White (the winning side) uses the <code>opponent</code> network;
    black (the defender) uses the <code>imitator</code>. Both have to
    have been initialised — if you've never visited the main page, click
    over there once first so the random-init weights get persisted.
  </p>

  <p>A few things worth poking at:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Crank sims/move up.</strong> At very low sims the search
      may not reach the mate; the visit count on <code>d1d7</code>
      (Qd7+) may be no larger than other queen moves. Increase sims/move
      and watch the visit count concentrate.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Play a non-mating move and look at the resulting $Q$.</strong>
      The new position should produce $Q$ values close to $0$ across
      candidates — the opponent's untrained value head doesn't know
      either side is materially ahead — but the leftover decisive lines
      still bias visits.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sweep cPuct.</strong> At $c_{\text{puct}} = 0.25$ you'll
      see the search lock onto one edge; at $c_{\text{puct}} = 4$ you'll
      see visits spread across many moves with $Q$ values barely
      moving from the prior order.
    </li>
  </ul>
`;

const outro = String.raw`
  <h2>8. Where this lives in the code</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>nn-chess/core/mcts/index.ts</code> — the entire search.
      <code>SearchResult</code> exposes <code>visitCounts</code>,
      <code>priorPolicy</code>, and <code>edgeValues</code> ($Q$ for
      each root edge); the table above is rendered from those.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>nn-chess/core/opponent/index.ts</code> —
      <code>policyFromNet</code> adapts a <code>ChessNet</code> to the
      <code>Policy</code> interface MCTS consumes; <code>selectMove</code>
      runs the search and applies the temperature sampler.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>nn-chess/ui/opponent-config.ts</code> — where the production
      defaults for sims/move, cPuct, and Dirichlet noise live.
    </li>
  </ul>
`;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="nn-chess — how MCTS works"
  description="A technical walk-through of PUCT-style Monte Carlo Tree Search, the sims/move and cPuct knobs, plus an interactive mate-in-2 demo run on your own opponent and imitator networks."
  useMath={true}
  pwa="nn-chess"
>
  <Fragment set:html={intro} />

  <div style="max-width: 760px; margin: 16px auto 0 auto;">
    <MctsExplainer client:only="react" />
  </div>

  <Fragment set:html={outro} />
</BaseLayout>
